## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of on-device, privacy-preserving ML systems with per-domain adapters that load dynamically while maintaining strict privacy guarantees in a chat application context.

## Key Concepts
- On-device fine-tuning with per-domain adapters
- Dynamic adapter loading from secure knowledge bases
- Adapter injection points (attention vs FFN layers)
- Quantization trade-offs (4-bit vs 8-bit)
- Domain gating and routing strategies
- Memory budgeting for mobile deployment
- Cross-domain evaluation and privacy compliance

## Code Example
```javascript
// Adapter hub loads domain-specific adapters dynamically
class AdapterHub {
  async loadAdapter(domain) {
    const adapter = await this.secureKB.fetch(domain);
    this.activeAdapters.set(domain, adapter);
  }

  route(query) {
    return this.gatingModel.predict(query);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of attention scaling, memory constraints, and practical trade-offs in production-grade transformers.

## Key Concepts
- Block-local attention and memory scaling
- Attention masking within a window
- Q/K/V reshaping for block processing
- Trade-offs: accuracy vs latency vs memory

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: local attention in blocks
function localAttention(Q, K, V, block=256, window=128){
  // Q,K,V: [B, T, D]
  const B = Q.length, T = Q[0].length, D = Q[0][0].length;
  let O = Array.from({length:B}, () => Array.from({length:T}, () => new Array(D).fill(0)));
  for (let b=0; b<B; b++){
    for (let s=0; s<T; s+=block){
      const end = Math.min(T, s+block);
      for (let t=s; t<end; t++){
        const wStart = Math.max(0, t-window);
        const wEnd = Math.min(T, t+window+1);
        // compute attention over [wStart, wEnd)
        // A = softmax((Q[t]·K[w].T)/sqrt(D))
        // O[b][t] = sum_{w=wStart}^{wEnd-1} A[w]·V[w]
      }
    }
  }
  return O;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does changing window size affect long-range dependency capture and memory?
- What metrics would you monitor to detect degradation when switching from global to local attention?

## Why This Is Asked
To evaluate ability to design end-to-end retrieval systems with multilingual data, latency constraints, and production-grade maintenance. The candidate must articulate chunking, embeddings, indexing, reranking, provenance, and monitoring trade-offs in a realistic enterprise setting.

## Key Concepts
- Retrieval-augmented generation
- Embeddings for multilingual data
- Vector databases and indexing strategies (IVF+PQ, HNSW)
- Document chunking and provenance
- Reranking with cross-encoders
- Drifts, updates, caching, latency budgets
- Evaluation metrics: recall@k, QA accuracy

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: initial retrieval then rerank
function retrieveAnswer(query, docs){
  const chunks = chunkDocs(docs);
  const emb = embed(query);
  const candidates = vectorDB.search(emb, topK=10);
  const ranked = reranker.rank(query, candidates);
  return formatAnswer(ranked[0]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you monitor embedding drift across languages?
- How would you handle adding a new language with few examples?
- What metrics would you use to balance latency vs. accuracy?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical RAG design for payments, emphasizing chunking, embedding sizing, vector DB indexing, latency, and freshness controls in real-world contexts.

## Key Concepts

- Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)
- Document chunking and overlap strategies
- Vector databases (e.g., Pinecone, Weaviate)
- Re-ranking and freshness management

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode: chunk 4KB with 25% overlap
const CHUNK_SIZE = 4 * 1024;
const OVERLAP = Math.floor(CHUNK_SIZE * 0.25);
function chunk(doc) {
  const blocks = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < doc.length; i += CHUNK_SIZE - OVERLAP) {
    blocks.push(doc.slice(i, i + CHUNK_SIZE));
  }
  return blocks;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you monitor vector DB drift and trigger reindexing automatically?
- How would you evaluate retrieval quality in production (precision@K, user feedback)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests multi-tenant adapter design under strict hardware and privacy constraints; evaluates decision rationale for injection points, quantization, and evaluation rigor.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant adapter design and privacy guarantees
- Adapter types: LoRA, QLoRA, PEFT and their trade-offs
- Injection points: attention first, FFN fallback
- Quantization, step budgets, and gradient checkpointing
- Evaluation: latency, factual accuracy, privacy leakage tests

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-train step applying per-tenant adapters
function trainStep(batch, tenantId) {
  const baseOut = model.forward(batch.inputs);
  const adapOut = adapter[tenantId].apply(baseOut);
  const loss = lossFn(batch.targets, adapOut) + adapterReg(tenantId);
  optimizer.step(loss);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate tenant isolation in logs and metrics?
- What rollback strategy would you use if a tenant adapter regresses performance?
